Anton:

> Showing my ignorance:  In genetic programming, is there any advantage
> (besides speed) to literal imitation of meiotic crossover?  Why not use
> a random bit-string and its complement as masks?

Genetic Programming generally doesn't imitate real crossover, since it
swaps trees.  Traditional Genetic Algoritims often does, for simplicity,
but there are lots of different types of crossover.  The approach you
describe is essentially the same as  a commonly-used operator called
"uniform crossover", which seems to work better than other operators
on some problems.

