From: Scott C DeLancey <delancey@darkwing.uoregon.edu>
Subject: EVOL:rattlesnakes

On Thu, 1 Jul 1993, Martin R. Olah wrote:

> I saw no response to the rattesnake questions so I will take a stab.
> Neither the rattle nor the fangs are as evolutionarily unlikely as they may
> seem. I believe that the rattle is made of sand trapped within incompletely
> shed layers of skin(don't ask me how it forms so perfectly, or why all snakes
> don't have them).

Nope.  No sand, just skin.  But "incompletely shed" is probably the clue;
snakes slough off their skins from head to tail, and you sometimes see
them with bits of the old skin still hanging off the end of the tail.
Conceivably that could be enough of a noisemaker to be adaptive, in a
species that was already poisonous enough that predators would be better
off avoiding it.

> An intermetiate step for the evolution of hypodermic fangs 
> would be grooved teeth.

Right.  The original adaptation is digestive enzymes in the saliva
that start right in to work on living tissue (there's a body of 
anecdotal reports--mostly from herpetologists who end up getting
chewed on a lot--of noticeable reactions to the bites of large
non-poisonous snakes).  You see three stages of evolution of the
delivery mechanism in poisonous snakes:  the rear-fanged "semi-poisonous"
snakes (there are a couple of species in the Southwest; I don't
know if they occur anywhere else in the world) have small, grooved
fangs.  Snakes of the cobra family have small hollow "hypodermic"
fangs, and the vipers (including rattlesnakes) have large hinged
hollow fangs that lie flat in the mouth until the snake strikes.
     I think all three of these are independent parallel developments.
Another example of parallel evolution is the gila monster (a lizard), 
which has decidedly poisonous saliva, but no very effective delivery
mechanism, so that in the ordinary course of events one has to
chew on you for a while to have any impact.  (There used to be
geeks in the SW who capitalized on this by letting themselves be
bitten by gila monsters, in carnivals or to win bar bets.  Every
once in a while somebody's luck would run out ...)


