Date: Fri, 2 Jul 93 12:14:06 -0700
From: hfinney@shell.portal.com
Subject: AIT VirtSem: Purpose of AIT

From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)

> But I have a question for you, Hal:  why
> would you like AIT extended (if you would at all)?  That is, what
> question would you like to answer with an extended AIT?  Personally,
> my largest interest is rather practical: I'd like to do some sort of
> calculation of the difficulty of producing objects that have certain
> functional properties (doing classification tasks, thinking, self-
> reproducing, making some kind of widget, etc) -- by purposive or
> evolutionary means.  That's why this connection interests me.

I agree with this goal.  I am interested in such questions as: Can
evolution increase complexity?  Does it inevitably increase complexity?
What is life, and how could we recognize forms of life that are more
abstract than those we see around us?

More fundamentally, what are those things in the universe which are
worth valuing, and which may we freely destroy without regret?  I realize
that this is basically a personal decision, but I feel more comfortable
destroying matter which is simple, unorganized, and easily re-created than
matter which is complex and unusual.

> Suppose we do take "life" as some sort of thing that an "objective"
> AIT should be maximum for.  Well, I very much doubt that we'd even
> agree on what life is without bringing in functional concepts, so
> how are we to decide how well an AIT measure performs?

I think we should come up with an objective measure that matched our
intuition on those cases for which we were confident that we were right,
without having too many kludges built in just for that purpose.  The
more objective and abstract the measure is, the better.  Then we would
feel more confident in extending this measure to environments where our
own intuition is less to be trusted (alife programs, for example).

In my view, AIT is better thought of as providing a measure of randomness
than one of complexity or value.  A high-AI string is one which is very
random.  A low-AI string is one which is very non-random.  That's all.

Bennett, in the excerpt Tim uploaded, argued that logical depth is an
abstract measure of value.  This is very much like what I am looking for.
Something which is logically deep cannot be re-created from random
beginnings without a great deal of calculation.  This suggests that it may
be a candidate for something to be cherished and preserved, since if we
destroy it we can't get it back easily.

That doesn't particularly mean that it is a _useful_ object, of course,
but as Bennet says we wouldn't expect any measure to be so anthropomorphic.
Still, logically deep objects are items we should think twice before
destroying.

Are living things logically deep?  Does evolution increase the logical
depth of objects?  These are things I'd like to see discussed.

Hal

