Date: Fri, 2 Jul 93 12:14:05 -0700
From: hfinney@shell.portal.com
Subject: AIT VirtSem: Critical Bits, Structure, and Information

From: derek@cs.wisc.edu (Derek Zahn)

> First, as Tim has pointed out, we always take some "environmental"
> concerns into account in the nature of the UTM we choose to use
> to do the programming.  This pales to insignificance on infinite
> strings, but is of some real importance for finite strings.

This is true, and Robin raised the same concern, as did the poster on
alt.privacy.clipper.  How important is it that the AIT measure depends
on the choice of Turing machine?

Traditional complexity theory, which analyzes running time of various
computer algorithms, faces the same problem.  There it is conventional
to use "big-O" notation for describing the running time of, say, a
sorting algorithm.  This is meant to reflect the fact that the details
will depend on the implementation and machine, but there are still fundamental
limits on the running time.

Chaitin uses the concept of a "Universal" computer "U" which has the
property that for any computer C there is a CONSTANT sim(C) such that
for any program p runnable on C there is a program p' runnable on U
which produces the same output, and such that the size of p' is no more
than the size of p plus sim(C).  (This is a slight oversimplification
from his AIT, chapter 6, page 107.)

In other words, given U one can simulate any other computer C by adding
a FIXED-SIZE program which simulates C to the front (in effect) of any
program for C.

Chaitin (in this book, anyway) then picks a specific U, and uses that
for his measure of complexity.

Choosing a U is thus analogous to choosing the 0 of a temperature scale.
Other people may choose different U's, differnt 0's, and so they will
get different answers for temperature, but that doesn't mean that the
notion is poorly defined.

(In my opinion, a more serious challenge to the validity of AIT measure
is that it cannot be calculated, that we can never determine what the
actual AIT measure is of a large string.  That is because we can't know
which is truly the shortest program to output that string, due to the
unsolvability of the halting problem.)

I reply to Derek's more general point in another message.

Hal

