Date: Fri, 2 Jul 93 12:14:03 -0700
From: hfinney@shell.portal.com
Subject: AIT VirtSem: Undecidable vs. unprovable

-----BEGIN PGP SIGNED MESSAGE-----

Note that if Fermat's theorem is unprovable, then this implies that if
we try all possible integer quadruplets x, y, z, n (n>2), in order, we will
never find a set such that x^n + y^n = z^n.  For if we did, then someone
could disprove Fermat's theorem by eventually discovering these values,
therefore the theorem would not be unprovable.

As I understand it, if Fermat's theorem is unprovable within the standard
axioms of arithmetic (i.e. it is independent of the axioms), that means
that we can append it as an axiom.  But it also means that we can append
its _opposite_ as an axiom equally as well.   

This means that we can say that there is some x, y, z, n, with n>2, 
such that x^n + y^n = z^n, with n, x, y, and z all being integers.  This
axiom can be conjoined with the standard axioms of number theory, and we
will have a perfectly consistent system.

How do we reconcile this fact with the fact stated above that no such
x, y, z, n will ever be found?

I asked about this on a math list about three years ago, and I was told
that there was no inconsistency.  Instead, the x, y, z and n which satisfy
the axiom are kind of different from what we would normally think of as
integers - they are integers which we would never get to by counting. 
Nevertheless, they fully satisfy all of the axioms of number theory which
implicitly define the integers.   

This can be viewed as showing that our axioms are not strong enough, that 
they do not fully capture all of what we think of as integers.  We have
tried to narrow things down with our axioms, so we will all agree on what
we are talking about.  But we have failed; other things exist which match
all of our axioms, but still aren't like what we think of as integers.

(This is like plane geometry without the parallel postulate; we may think
we have defined what constitutes a plane, but without realizing it we have
allowed curved surfaces to satisfy our axioms and so we aren't dealing with
what we think we are dealing with.)

Godel's theorem says that we are always doomed to such failures; that no
matter how we try to pin down what we talk about, there will still be more
mathematical objects that satisfy all our rules but which don't behave the
way we think they should.

Hal

