Here's the main "References and Source Materials" list from the Internet
Witch Trial report (not yet distributed).

Several people have requested this, and at least one person on the List
somewhat churlishly wrote "the research that Tim claims to have done." with
no smileys attached, so this may help in some way counter these claims that
the research was a sham.

(The formatting is strange, because I'm going from an outliner (MORE) to
text. Please, don't send suggestions on how to reformat...I just don't have
time right now to fool with "pretty-printing" this stuff.)



+       References and Source Materials

-       Appleyard, Bryan, "Understanding the Present: Science and the Soul
of Modern Man," Doubleday, 1993.
-       Bartley, W.W., III, "The Retreat to Committment," 2nd ed., Open
Court, 1984. His flavor of evolutionary epistemology is influential amongst
free market advocates, while others tend to dismiss him.
-       Beckwith, Martha, "Hawaiian Mythology," Univ. of Hawaii Press,
1940, 1970.  
-       Bonewits, P.E.I., "Real Magic: An Introductory Treatise on the
Basic Principles of Yellow Magic," (Revised edition), Creative Arts Book
Company, 1971, 1979. The first (and only?) person to receive a degree in
magic, this from UC Berkeley. Starts out OK, but devolves into ghosts,
ouija boards, and ESP.
-       Carroll, Peter J., "Liber Kaos," Samuel Weiser, Inc., 1992.
Explicates his "Chaos Magic Theory." A confusing mix of Qabalism, quantum
mechanics, many worlds, etc. The "dark side"of magick (something Adler
mostly dismisses).
-       Carroll, Peter J., "Liber Null and Psychonaut: An Introduction to
Chaos Magic," Samuel Weiser, 1987. Baphomet, chaos, quantum theory, and the
Illuminates of Thanateros.
-       Conway, D.J., "Norse Magic," Llewellyn Publications, 1990. Mostly
worthless...I just cited it for a quote from the preface. (Most Llewellyn
books are hastily written and sensationalized.)
-       Crowley, Aleister, "The Law is for All: An Extended Commentary on
the Book of the Law," edited by Israel Regardie, New Falcon Publishing,
1991. The introduction and preface are interesting, the Crowley part I
couldn't read (numerology, obscure Egyptian mythology, Nuit, etc,). Maybe
you have to be on drugs to read it.
-       Crowley, Vivianne, "Wicca: The Old Religion in the New Age," The
Aquarian Press, 1989. A modern witch with a somewhat more extreme view
(ghosts, ESP, divining, the astral plane) than popularizers like Adler and
Starhawk.
-       Davis, Wade, "The Serpent and the Rainbow," Simon and Schuster,
1985. Anthropologist and ethnobotanist goes to Haiti to look for zombie
poison.
-       Dawkins, Richard, "The Awe Factor," in "The Skepical Inquirer,"
Vol. 17, Spring 1993.
-       Dawkins, Richard, "The Selfish Gene," Oxford University Press,
1976. And also his "The Blind Watchmaker," of course. Classics on how
natural processes are sufficient to explain the real world.
-       Eliade, Mircea, "The Sacred and the Profane: The Nature of
Religion," Harcourt Brace Jovanovich, 1959. A scholarly study of world
religions. His many books delve into the nature of religion, the common
myths and metaphors (such as the "World Tree" common to many religions),
and nature of the spirit-body dichotomy.
-       Gardner, Martin, "The New Age: Notes of a Fringe Watcher,"
Prometheus Books, 1988. Gardner is of course a leading skeptic and debunker
and has authored several books along these lines.
-       Graves, Robert, "The White Goddess," Farrar, Straus, and Giroux,
1948. The impetus for much of modern feminist Goddess worship, but
considered unreliable by modern scholars.
-       Griffin, David Ray, editor, "The Reenchantment of Science:
Postmodern Proposals," SUNY Press, 1988. More on the "unmagicking" of the
modern world and how it can be combatted.
-       Huber, Peter, "Galileo's Revenge: Junk Science in the Courtroom,"
Basic Books, 1991. Very skeptical of claims of the paranormal, of psychics
who lose their powers after being CAT-scanned, etc.
-       Kelly, Aidin, "An Update on Neopagan Witchcraft in America," in
Lewis, James R. and Melton, J. Gordon, editors, "Perspectives on the New
Age," SUNY Press, 1992. Kelly was the founder of the New Reformed Orthodox
Order of the Golden Dawn, NROOGD. He has since returned to the Catholic
Church and then back again to Paganism and Wicca (last I heard!). I find
his point to be about the best I've seen (closest to me, that is), and if I
were to read more on Paganism, Kelly is where I'd look next.
-       Lewis, James R. and Melton, J. Gordon, editors, "Perspectives on
the New Age," SUNY Press, 1992.
-       Luhrmann, T.M., "Persuasions of the Witch's Craft: Ritual Magic in
Contemporary England," Harvard University Press, 1989. A sociological
treatment of modern British witches. Dry reading.
-       Parrinder, Geoffrey, "African Mythology," Peter Bedrick Books,
1967, 1982.
-       Putnam, Hilary, "Representation and Reality," MIT Press, 1988.
Refutes his own "functionalism" model and sees limits on the computational
model of the mind.
-       Radnitzky, Gerard, and W. W. Bartley, III, editors, "Evolutionary
Epistemology, Rationality, and the Sociology of Knowledge," Open
Court,1987. Several chapters on "pan rational criticism" (PCR).
-       Rae, Alexander C., "Bluff Your Way in the Occult," Ravette Books,
U.K., 1988. A nice little synopsis of occult and witchly terms.
-       Raymond, Eric, editor,  "The New Hacker's Dictionary." MIT Press, 1991.
-       Starhawk (Miriam Simos), "Dreaming the Dark: Magic, Sex and
Politics," Beacon Press, 1982. A screed against nuclear power and the evils
of modern technology. Starhawk has gone completely over to the
feminist/activist/antinuclear side and is no longer very interesting on
Pagan issues.
-       Starhawk (Miriam Simos), "The Spiral Dance: A Rebirth of the
Ancient Religion of the Great Goddess," Harper and Row, 1979. Very
influential in triggering the surge in popularity of witchcraft, especially
amongst feminists. (Similar to "Z. Budapest," an LA witch who is also an
ardent feminist/lesbian/activist.)
-       Tarnas, Richard, "The Passion of the Western Mind: Understanding
the Ideas that have Shaped Our World View," Ballantine Books, 1991. An
excellent survey of Western thought, with the Promethean-Gaian axis taking
center stage. His conclusions are debatable, though.
-       Weinstein, Marion, "Earth Magic: A Dianic Book of Shadows," Phoenix
Publishing, 1980. See next entry for comments.
-       Weinstein, Marion, "Positive Magic: Occult Self-Help," Revised
Edition, Phoenix Publishing, 1981. Rituals, the deities, sacred objects,
astral travel, reincarnation, familiars (cats and dogs), divination and
scrying, contacting departed spirits.
-       Wilson, Robert Anton, "The New Inquisition: Irrational Rationalism
and the Citadel of Science," New Falcon Publications, 1986, 1991. Wilson is
a classic discordian, playful, puckish writer. His book "Sex, Drugs, and
Rock and Roll" (may not be the exact title), has a good opening chapter on
Crowley's magick, which I started looking at very late in the writing of
this report, and so have not emphasized.


