Hal Finney writes:

>From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
>> But AIT _cannot_ avoid the embedding context (my term).
>
>I don't follow this.  I view AIT as not requiring any embedding for a
>string in order to give an information measure, in contrast to traditional
>IT.  This avoidance of a context is what I was arguing was worth keeping.

I think this is incorrect, though I'm certainly willing to have my thinking
straightened out on this.

An arbitrary string that is to be "compressed" (have a shorter description
found) must have some computational model which can find the "structure." A
very primitive compressor (a simple program) might only try "run-length
compression," exploiting runs of 1s or 0s to achieve some slight
compression.

(I say "slight" because, for randomly picked strings, the compression
achieved by run-length compression (any compression, actually, but that's a
broader issue) averages out to be zero...some strings get shorter, some get
longer.)

A "smarter" program might look for things like the string being the binary
expansion of some easy-to-describe number.

And a "genius" program might look for even more clever compressions. That
is, much shorter descriptions. (That most arbitrary strings don't have
shorter descriptions is a different matter, dealt with in the proceeding
post of mine.)


>
>> (Interesting parallels to Maxwell's Demon and "putting all the molecules on
>> one side of the box," so to speak, (Sorry if this allusion is unclear.) We
>> end up forming a highly "improbable" state, in classical IT terms, just as
>> Dawkins describes in "The Blind Watchmaker." Call it "Darwin's Demon."
>> Hmmhhh...maybe I ought to patent that name!)
>
>I don't follow this analogy; perhaps Tim could enlarge upon it at some
>point.

Evolution creates highly improbable configurations, or strings, which are
not found by purely random arrangements. Hence the analogy with Maxwell's
Demon, as in the celebrated example of the chances that all the gas
molecules in a box will randomly move and find themselves in the same half
of the box.

(With 10 molecules, this'll happen fairly frequently--one in a thousand of
all "snapshots" of the box will show all the molecules in the same side of
the box--but with 10^6 or 10^10 or mole quantitied (macroscopic volumes),
it'll essentially never happen.)

"Darwin's Demon" uses evolution, survival of the fittest (whatever that
means, exactly!), differential reproduction, fitness landscapes, etc., so
as to produce organisms described by strings (genomes, genomes of competing
organisms, embyrological issues, etc....that is, more than just genome of
one organism) that are very much different from "random" strings.

I think this point is very well-established in the evolution community, in
the books like "The Blind Watchmaker," and in the GA/GP community.


-Tim 

