Date: Fri, 2 Jul 93 15:09:25 -0700
From: tcmay@netcom.com (Timothy C. May)
Subject: AIT VirtSem: Critical Bits, Structure, and Information

>(In my opinion, a more serious challenge to the validity of AIT measure
>is that it cannot be calculated, that we can never determine what the
>actual AIT measure is of a large string.  That is because we can't know
>which is truly the shortest program to output that string, due to the
>unsolvability of the halting problem.)

>Hal

That AIT cannot actually calculate the "measure" (such as the shortest
program that output) of a given string is not a challenge for AIT, but
implicit in the nature of strings.

If I give you the string "101000100110100101101001101100001010010000101"
and ask you for the shortest description, how would you proceed?

- compression will sometimes produce a slightly stronger string (and
sometimes, but of course rarely, a _much_ shorter string, or description).
But compression will just as often produce a _longer_ string, unless
there's a lot of "redundancy" (structure, order, autocorrelation, whatever
you want to call it) in the string. Compression works for such "nonrandom"
things as English (2.3 bits per character...a lot of structure), as images
(lots of white space, continuous patches of some color, etc.), and so
forth, but not for arbitrarily picked strings.

(A compelling way to look at this: for arbitrary ("random") 1000-bit
strings, for example, at at most one in a thousand of them can be
compressed to 990 bits or less, and so on. There just aren't "enough bits
of equivocation" to do better than that. Pigeohole prinicple, and all.)

- by great cleverness, some Marilyn vos Savant AI program crunches on this
string and announces that can be compressed (described) in, say 27 bits
instead of the 45 bits shown above. Perhaps it is the square root of
43,998,865 epxressed in binary, perhaps it's the population of the Earth in
binary

[this touches on the issue of the Turing Machine one picks, in the sense of
relating a string to some externally-extant number or fact....lots of tough
issues here.]

- but can this vos Savant program "prove" that there is no shorter
description, perhaps of 7 bits?

- the answer, proved by Kolmogorov, Solomonoff, and Chaitin, in varying
ways, is "No."

As Hal noted, this is equivalent to the Halting Problem.

But if Hal is criticizing AIT for not even providing some clues as to how
to get "satisficing" answers, good enough answers, then there is some
validity to this. Ironically, AIT is not "rich enough" (in AIT terms!) to
provided methodologies for generating candidate shorter
descriptions...that's more the field of pattern recognition (a form of
AIT-style compression), the kind of Bayesian scene analysis that Robin
Hanson has been doing (examining fuzzy or blurry images, perhaps with
several different angles or sensors and then "deducing" or finding a
Occam's Razor-like "description" that requires minimal assumptions...a kind
of AIT-style "shorter description"), and so on.

Nearly all of language, scene recognition, and so on, is this kind of
replacing of long bit strings (acoustic waveforms, images, sensory
impressions) with "shorter" descriptions (words, faces, smells, etc.).

AIT per se is not a set of tools for doing this, it's an abstract (but
foundational, IMHO) way of viewing bounds and fundamental capabilities,
just as conventional information theory was but one part of the panoply of
tools needed to do actual work.

-Tim

